      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the tartare sauce"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix {{mayonnaise}} of mayonnaise with {{capers}} of capers roughly chopped, {{gherkins}} gherkins finely chopped, and {{lemon-juice}} of lemon juice in a small bowl. Season and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bread and fry the fish fingers"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Set up three shallow bowls: one with flour seasoned with salt, one with the beaten egg, one with panko breadcrumbs. Season the {{cod-fillet}} of fish strips, then pass each one through flour, egg, and breadcrumbs in that order, pressing the crumbs on firmly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a frying pan over medium-high heat. When the oil shimmers, add the fish fingers in a single layer. Fry for 3 to 4 minutes per side until deep golden and cooked through. Do not crowd the pan: fry in batches if needed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain on kitchen paper for a minute, then season with salt."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Build the sandwich"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spread tartare sauce generously on two slices of bread. Lay the fish fingers on top, then add shredded lettuce. Close with the second slice and press gently."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "symptom": "Breadcrumbs fall off during frying",
              "cause": "Fish was wet when breaded, or the flour coat was skipped",
              "fix": "Pat the fish completely dry before starting. Do not skip the flour stage; it is the glue that holds the egg, which holds the breadcrumbs."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Inside still raw when breadcrumbs are dark",
              "cause": "Pan too hot, or fish cut too thick",
              "fix": "Use medium rather than high heat, and cut the fish no thicker than 2 cm. The crust should take 3 to 4 minutes per side to colour."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Crust is greasy and heavy",
              "cause": "Oil not hot enough when the fish fingers went in",
              "fix": "The oil must be shimmering hot before the fish fingers go in. Cold oil soaks into the crust rather than frying it."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Sandwich becomes soggy before it is finished",
              "cause": "Too much sauce or fish fingers not drained after frying",
              "fix": "Drain the fish fingers on kitchen paper for a minute. Add tartare sauce to the bread, not directly onto the hot fish."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The fish finger sandwich became a cultural fixture in Britain partly because everyone grew up with the packet version. The homemade version makes the same argument the fish finger always made: white fish in crisp breadcrumbs is a good combination, and it does not need to be complicated. It is now on the menu at good pubs and delis as an unironic sandwich, served with chunky tartare sauce."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}